# Conflicting control rules, bounded search and counterexample replay

## Goal

Show the boundary where formal control verification belongs: between the
numeric proposals `wotex_nx` decodes and the consumer decision that may
dispatch one of them. The Lab's decision policy already refuses conflicting
rules, stale observations, revoked and expired grants, and it never lets a
verifier result grant authority. The `ex_maude` profile of WLB.09 that would
explore modeled transition orders, prove exhaustion or produce a replayable
counterexample is **planned**: it is fully specified, has no source in this
package, and no cell below pretends otherwise.

The notebook's evidence status is `partial`: the policy cells run against
the cohort; the bounded search and counterexample replay are documentation
only and are marked as such.

## Prerequisites

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:wotex_lab, "~> 0.1.0"},
  {:nx, "~> 0.13.1"}
])
```

The requirements above are published-artifact requirements. At the time this
notebook was written none of the `wotex*` packages is published on Hex; the
notebook is verified against the workspace source cohort recorded in
`docs/provenance/source-cohort.json` by `test/wotex/lab/cookbook_test.exs`,
which evaluates every Elixir cell below in order (skipping this install
cell). `Mix.install/1` never points at a Git checkout, and it deliberately
does not list `ex_maude`: no Maude binary is downloaded or started by this
notebook or by the base library.

## Package and consumer ownership

| Concern | Owner | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Decoded proposals | `wotex_nx` (package-implements) | `Wotex.Nx.Decoder`, `ActionProposal` |
| Decision binding, conflict refusal, dispatch once, revoke, records | `wotex_lab` (consumer) | `Wotex.Lab.SmartRoom.Policy` |
| The simulated effect | `wotex_lab` reference host | `Wotex.Lab.Reference.Thing` |
| Finite control model, bounded search, exhaustion basis, counterexample replay | planned `ex_maude` profile (WLB.09) | not in source |
| Formal verification never granting an Action | consumer policy, by construction | `Policy.dispatch/4` takes no verifier input |

## Run

```elixir
alias Wotex.DataSchema
alias Wotex.Lab
alias Wotex.Lab.Adapters.Runtime.{Loopback, StaticRef}
alias Wotex.Lab.Error, as: LabError
alias Wotex.Lab.Reference.Thing
alias Wotex.Lab.SmartRoom.Policy
alias Wotex.Lab.Telemetry
alias Wotex.Nx.{Decoder, OutputSchema}
alias Wotex.Runtime.{BindingProfile, ConsumedThing, Context, Result}
alias Wotex.ThingDescription

telemetry_id = "cookbook-formal-control-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

events =
  for component <- Telemetry.components(),
      operation <- Telemetry.operations(),
      event <- [:start, :stop, :exception],
      do: [:wotex, :lab, component, operation, event]

:ok = :telemetry.attach_many(telemetry_id, events, &Telemetry.forward/4, self())
```

The actuator and the policy. The policy allows `:operator` to set targets
between 5 and 35; every decision binds the proposal digest, the observation
watermark and the simulated state revision.

```elixir
{:ok, lab} = Lab.start_link(id: "formal-control", max_children: 8)
{:ok, td} = Application.app_dir(:wotex_lab, "priv/fixtures/loopback/thing-description.json") |> File.read!() |> ThingDescription.parse()
thing_id = ThingDescription.id(td)

{:ok, actuator} =
  Lab.start_child(
    lab,
    :things,
    {Thing,
     td: td,
     state: %{"temperature" => 20.0, "target" => 21.0},
     tokens: %{"bearer_sc" => "room-token"},
     actions: %{
       "setTarget" => fn input, state -> {:ok, %{"accepted" => true}, :accepted, Map.put(state, "target", input)} end
     }}
  )

{:ok, profile} = BindingProfile.new(id: :loopback, schemes: ["loopback"], operations: Wotex.Runtime.operations())

{:ok, consumed} =
  ConsumedThing.new(td,
    profiles: [profile],
    transports: %{loopback: {Loopback, %{host: actuator}}},
    credentials: {StaticRef, %{references: %{"bearer_sc" => "ref"}, lookup: fn "ref" -> {:ok, "room-token"} end}}
  )

{:ok, policy} =
  Lab.start_child(lab, :sessions, {Policy, id: :policy, allowed: [:operator], limits: %{"setTarget" => %{min: 5, max: 35}}})

{:ok, bounds} = DataSchema.new(%{"type" => "number", "minimum" => 5, "maximum" => 35})

{:ok, output} =
  OutputSchema.new(
    kind: :action_proposal,
    thing_id: thing_id,
    affordance_type: :action,
    affordance_name: "setTarget",
    data_schema: bounds,
    dtype: :f32
  )

proposal = fn id, value ->
  {:ok, proposal} = Decoder.decode(Nx.tensor(value, type: :f32), output, id: id, proposed_at: 1_000)
  proposal
end

dispatcher = fn value ->
  fn -> ConsumedThing.invoke_action(consumed, "setTarget", value, Context.new!(request_id: "formal-" <> to_string(value))) end
end

Thing.stats(actuator).handler_calls
```

Conflicting rules: a comfort rule proposes `24.0`, an energy rule proposes
`18.0` for the same Thing and Action at the same watermark. The first grant
is outstanding, so the second is refused as a conflict rather than queued;
the granted decision dispatches once.

```elixir
comfort = proposal.("comfort-1", 24.0)
energy = proposal.("energy-1", 18.0)
decision_context = [principal: :operator, watermark: 1, state_revision: 1, now: 1_000, ttl: 5_000]

{:ok, granted} = Policy.decide(policy, comfort, decision_context)
{:error, %LabError{code: conflict}} = Policy.decide(policy, energy, decision_context)

{:ok, {:ok, %Result{status: dispatched}}} =
  Policy.dispatch(policy, granted.id, [now: 1_001, watermark: 1, state_revision: 1], dispatcher.(24.0))

{:error, %LabError{code: second}} =
  Policy.dispatch(policy, granted.id, [now: 1_002, watermark: 1, state_revision: 1], dispatcher.(24.0))

{conflict, dispatched, second, Thing.stats(actuator)}
```

Once the comfort decision is dispatched, the energy rule can be decided at
the next watermark; revoking it before dispatch leaves the actuator alone. A
grant that is never dispatched stays outstanding and would block the next
decision for the same Thing and Action, so it is revoked explicitly too.

```elixir
{:ok, energy_grant} = Policy.decide(policy, energy, Keyword.merge(decision_context, watermark: 2, now: 2_000))
:ok = Policy.revoke(policy, energy_grant.id)

{:error, %LabError{code: revoked}} =
  Policy.dispatch(policy, energy_grant.id, [now: 2_001, watermark: 2, state_revision: 1], dispatcher.(18.0))

{:ok, stale_grant} = Policy.decide(policy, proposal.("energy-2", 18.0), Keyword.merge(decision_context, watermark: 3, now: 3_000))

{:error, %LabError{code: stale}} =
  Policy.dispatch(policy, stale_grant.id, [now: 3_001, watermark: 4, state_revision: 1], dispatcher.(18.0))

:ok = Policy.revoke(policy, stale_grant.id)
{revoked, stale, Thing.stats(actuator)}
```

The bounded search and counterexample replay of WLB.09, shown as
documentation only (this block is not an Elixir cell and is not executed):

```text
# Planned (WLB.09, implementation_status: planned; ex_maude profile).
# pool  = ExMaude.Pool.child_spec(name: pool_name, executable: verified_maude_path, size: 1)
# model = %{id: "thermal-control-v1", digest: "sha256:..."}   # checked-in, digest-addressed
# query = "search [1] in THERMAL-CONTROL : init(bands, modes, decisions) =>* unsafe(S) ."
# {:ok, result} = Wotex.Lab.Formal.search(pool, model, query, max_depth: 100, solutions: 1, deadline_ms: 5_000, max_output_bytes: 1_048_576)
# result.status in [:counterexample, :verified_in_model, :inconclusive, :timeout, :unsupported, :error]
# A `:counterexample` replays as an ordered finite simulator trace against the smart room;
# a bounded search with no solution is `:inconclusive` unless exhaustion is proven.
# Neither outcome touches Thing.stats(actuator).handler_calls.
```

## Deliberate breakage

Anything a verifier could say is irrelevant to authority: the policy's
dispatch takes a decision id and the current watermark and revision, not a
verification result. Here a "verified" flag is simply not an input, and a
dispatch under a changed state revision is refused as stale state.

```elixir
verified_in_model = true

{:ok, revision_grant} =
  Policy.decide(policy, proposal.("comfort-2", 24.0), Keyword.merge(decision_context, watermark: 5, now: 5_000))

{:error, %LabError{code: stale_state}} =
  Policy.dispatch(policy, revision_grant.id, [now: 5_001, watermark: 5, state_revision: 2], dispatcher.(24.0))

calls_after = Thing.stats(actuator).handler_calls
records = Policy.records(policy)
{verified_in_model, stale_state, calls_after, Enum.map(records.refusals, & &1.reason), Enum.map(records.decisions, & &1.status)}
```

```elixir
:ok = Supervisor.stop(lab)
```

## Safe telemetry

Only the granted and current decision produced a dispatch span; refusals
produce none. The span carries a non-reversible `thing_ref` and the Action
name, not the Thing id or the input.

```elixir
drain = fn drain ->
  receive do
    {:wotex_lab_telemetry, event, measurements, metadata} ->
      [{event, measurements, metadata} | drain.(drain)]
  after
    0 -> []
  end
end

spans = drain.(drain)
:telemetry.detach(telemetry_id)

dispatches = for {[:wotex, :lab, :policy, :dispatch, :stop], _measurements, metadata} <- spans, do: metadata
text = inspect(spans)
labels_safe? = length(dispatches) == 1 and not (text =~ thing_id) and not (text =~ "24.0")
{dispatches, labels_safe?}
```

## Expected output

| Check | Expectation |
| --- | --- |
| `conflicting-rule-refused` | The energy proposal at the same watermark is `:conflicting_decision` |
| `dispatch-once` | The comfort decision dispatches once (`:accepted`); a second attempt is `:already_dispatched` |
| `revoked-inert` | A revoked grant is `:revoked` at dispatch and changes nothing |
| `stale-observation-inert` | A grant dispatched under a newer watermark is `:stale_observation`; a changed revision is `:stale_state` |
| `action-counter-unchanged` | The actuator saw exactly one handler call across every refusal |

```elixir
%{
  "conflicting-rule-refused" => conflict == :conflicting_decision,
  "dispatch-once" => dispatched == :accepted and second == :already_dispatched,
  "revoked-inert" => revoked == :revoked,
  "stale-observation-inert" => stale == :stale_observation and stale_state == :stale_state,
  "action-counter-unchanged" => calls_after == 1 and labels_safe?
}
```

## Public seam

`lab.policy` is Lab-owned: the consumer decision boundary between decoded
proposals and effects. `lab.formal_verification` is the planned WLB.09
seam; it has no module in this package and is recorded as planned in the
generated graph.

## Spec and completion IDs

* Lab: WLB.09, implementation status planned (`WLB-C09`, `WLB-C08`).
* Upstream: none claimed; `ex_maude` is not part of the source cohort.

## Replacement adapter

A replacement policy must bind at least the proposal digest, principal,
watermark, state revision and expiry, refuse conflicts instead of queueing
them, dispatch at most once and record refusals separately from dispatch
acknowledgements. When the `ex_maude` profile ships, its result is an
additional input to the decision, never to the dispatch.
